3DIR v3.1.1                                              John Elliott, 6-3-2001
===============================================================================

  3DIR is a drop-in replacement for the CP/M Plus DIR utility. It has the
following features:

* Program is 12k in size; original CP/M DIR is 15k.
* All options from the original DIR are valid; more options and display 
 formats are supported.
* Piping and redirection of output (via PIPEMGR) is supported. If this is
 not required, you can remove the RSX with:

   GENCOM 3DIR

  which will reduce program size to 8k.

New in this version:
====================

* Options can be separated by commas or white space, as in the original
  DIR:

    3DIR [DRIVE=B USER=4, EXCLUDE] *.SYM

Differences from the original DIR
=================================

* Output redirection: You can use:

    3DIR {command} >filename    Write to file
    3DIR {command} >>filename   Append to existing file
    3DIR {command} |program     Send output through a PIPEMGR filter

    Filenames in the ">" and ">>" options can contain user numbers - eg:
   10A:file.typ  or  B6:list.txt. These options also accept the device
   names CON: LST: AUX: and NUL: 

* Command-line filenames can contain user numbers. For example: 
   3DIR C2:*.COM

* Some of the statistics 3DIR brings up seem to be different (eg: used/max
  directory entries). 3DIR's statistics agree with the original SHOW, not
  the original DIR.

* The "file attributes" column always shows all seven attributes  
  R,S,A,1,2,3,4 rather than just "R/O", "Sys" and "Arcv"

* File sizes are shown in bytes, not records. Bytewise file lengths are
 supported.

* Date output is fully Year 2000 compliant and uses a 4-digit year. 

* Here are all the options 3DIR accepts. Options marked # are new in 3DIR;
 options marked ! behave differently in 3DIR.

  1       #Show only files that have the F1 attribute set
  2       #Show only files that have the F2 attribute set
  3       #Show only files that have the F3 attribute set
  4       #Show only files that have the F4 attribute set
  ARC      Show only files that have the Archive attribute set
  ATT     !Show filename, size, and attributes
            In DIR, this behaved like [FULL] but displayed all seven 
            attributes rather than the normal three. 3DIR always displays
            seven attributes.
  BARE    #Show filenames in the format ud:filename.ext. This is intended for
           use with PIPEMGR, to generate lists of files for further 
           processing.
  COLS    #Show filenames in columns, like the built-in DIR command
  DATE    !Show filename and dates.
            DIR treated this as a synonym for [FULL].
  DESC    #Sort in descending order
  DIR      Show only files that don't have the System attribute set
  DOS     #Display like the DRDOS "DIR /L" listing
  DOS2    #Display like the DRDOS "DIR /2" listing 
  DRIVE=   List of drives to use; either DRIVE=x:  DRIVE=ALL  or 
           DRIVE=(a,b,c)
  EXCLUDE  Filenames on the command line will be taken as files _not_ to
           list, rather than filenames to list
  FF       Print a form feed character after a heading
  FULL     Display filename, size, attributes, password mode and time stamps.
  G        Gn is shorthand for USER=n
  LENGTH= !Number of lines on a page. Every "length" lines, a heading will 
           be printed. 
             In DIR, this sets the length of the screen. In 3DIR, it is
           intended for use when the output is not displayed but is being
           passed through PIPEMGR.
  LS      #Do a listing like Unix "ls -l" or DRDOS "xdir"
  MESSAGE  Print a message when you're using USER= and DIR can't find any 
           files in a user area.
  NO PAGE  Don't pause every screenful.
  NO SORT  Don't sort results.
  ORDER=  #Sort order. This can be ORDER=NAME, ORDER=TYPE, ORDER=SIZE,
           ORDER=CREATE, ORDER=UPDATE, or ORDER=ACCESS (the last three
           referring to date stamps).
  PAGE     Do pause every screenful
  RO       List only read-only files
  RW       List only read/write files
  SIZE     List files and their sizes
  SYS      List only System files
  USER=    User areas to scan - either USER=n, USER=ALL or USER=(n,n,...)
  X1      #Don't show files that have the F1 attribute set
  X2      #Don't show files that have the F2 attribute set
  X3      #Don't show files that have the F3 attribute set
  X4      #Don't show files that have the F4 attribute set
  XA      #Don't show files that have the Archive attribute set

Customising
===========
  Thanks to the wonders of the GNU GPL, you should just be able to reassemble
3DIR. However, setting up the build process is fiddly, so some default values
can be changed without a rebuild.

  The settings area is at 0280h in the COM file (if you have removed PIPEMGR,
it will be at 0180h).

  Settings+0: Options area address
                If PIPEMGR has not been removed, you must at 0100h to this
                address.
  Settings+2: Column separator character for [COLS] and [SIZE] modes. Normally
             a colon.
  Settings+3: String to send to the printer (or other device) for a form feed.
             Up to 7 bytes followed by a zero. For example, you could make
             this your "clear screen" code and use 3DIR [FF] to clear the 
             screen at the start of every page of output.

The options area contains:
              
  Options+0: Sort order
             'N'= by name
             'T'= by type
             'S'= by size
             'C'= by create date
             'U'= by update date
             'A'= by access date
  Options+1: Output format
              0 = [FULL]
              1 = [ATT]
              2 = [SIZE]
              3 = [DATE]
              4 = [BARE]
              5 = [COLS]
              6 = [LS]
              7 = [DOS]
              8 = [DOS2]
  Options+2: Sort direction.
                0 = don't sort
                1 = normal sort
              0FFh= descending sort
